# Website writing style

Write like a founder/engineer sharing a clear thought — not a marketing team
launching a feature.

## Voice and tone

- Concise, clear, humanized. Short paragraphs.
- Natural and professional. No buzzwords ("revolutionary",
  "game-changing", "supercharge", "unlock", "leverage").
- No hype, no exaggerated claims, no emojis or decorative symbols.
- One clear idea or problem per post. Don't bundle multiple announcements.
- Authenticity > polish. Credibility > excitement.

## What to say (and not say)

- Cover one specific topic and stop. Resist the urge to preview the full
  roadmap or list every adjacent feature.
- Don't expose the full product vision early. Keep room for future posts.
- State uncertainty plainly ("on the roadmap", "we're exploring") rather
  than promising delivery.
- Prefer concrete examples and observable details to abstract claims.

## Formatting

- Headings: sentence case (`## Why we built this`, not `## Why We Built This`).
- Lists are fine, but don't list-ify everything; prefer prose where it reads
  better.
- Code fences and internal Markdown links work; no custom shortcodes.
- Bold sparingly — for the key noun in a sentence, not for emphasis spam.
- Length: aim for what the idea needs. ~250–500 words is usually right for a
  blog post; a doc page can be longer when the topic warrants it.

## Frontmatter checklist (blog)

```yaml
---
title: "Short, specific, no clickbait"
description: "One sentence summarising the post"
pubDate: 2026-05-18
author: "The CloudoX team"
tags: ["one", "two", "three"]
draft: false
---
```

`description` is shown on the index card and in `<meta>` — write it like a
human would describe the post, not like an SEO blurb.

## Audience

Both technical (consultants, engineers, architects) and non-technical
(decision-makers reading the marketing site). Explain just enough for the
non-technical reader without diluting the technical core.

## Confidentiality

Some details belong to the internal knowledge of how CloudoX works and
should not be exposed on the public website. Discovery strategy, filtering
heuristics, deduplication rules, prompt engineering specifics, performance
numbers, cost breakdowns, customer environment data, internal benchmarks,
roadmap timing, and competitive positioning are confidential by default.
When a post touches on how the product works:

- **Generalize, don't itemize.** Talk about principles ("we combine deep
  and broad collection") rather than mechanisms ("we deny-list these
  CloudFormation types and cap at N per region").
- **Name only shipped, publicly known capabilities.** If a feature is not
  already discoverable from the public README, the public GitHub repo, or
  the marketing site, do not name it on the website yet.
- **No internal data.** Do not share customer environment sizes, internal
  performance numbers, cost breakdowns, or roadmap dates.
- **Skip the cookbook.** Specific service inventories, filter rules,
  identifier mappings, allow/deny lists, type-coverage lists, and
  competitive comparisons stay inside the engineering repo.

When in doubt, omit the detail. A post can still be useful at the level
of "what changed and why it matters" without the underlying mechanism.

## Quick sanity check before publishing

- Could a real founder send this to a customer without it sounding generated?
- Is there exactly one takeaway?
- Did I avoid promising features that aren't shipped?
- Did I cut every adjective that doesn't earn its place?
- Did I generalize internal mechanisms rather than exposing them?
